If you already have an account to the 84.88.52.232 server you should access through ssh connection:

        $ ssh -X user@84.88.52.232

Then you should put the following commands to set-up the vivado enviroment:

        $ source /opt/Xilinx/Vivado/2018.1/settings64.sh

        $ source /opt/Xilinx/Vivado/2018.1/settings64.sh

        $ export XILINXD_LICENSE_FILE=4100@bsc-caos-gw.bsc.es

Then you can access to the FPGA by two ways

Using the Vivado GUI:

       $ vivado &

Now you can use vivado GUI to create and synthesize a project or use the hardware manager to program the FPGA.

The second way is to use tcl scripts to create a project, generate a bitstream and program the FPGA, I attached some examples of these scripts.

    - To create a project you should run the following

          $  vivado -mode batch -source make_project.tcl -tclargs "project_name" "ARG1" "ARG2"...

    - To synthesize and generate the project:

          $ vivado -mode batch -source make_bitstream.tcl -tclargs "project_name"

    - To program the FPGA:

            $ vivado -mode batch -source program.tcl -tclargs "xc7k325t_0" "project_name/project_name.runs/impl_1/Top_module.bit"


If you have access to the lagarto-lowrisc repository, https://repo.hca.bsc.es/gitlab/lagarto/lagarto-lowrisc, you can run it as an example.

    $ git clone https://repo.hca.bsc.es/gitlab/lagarto/lagarto-lowrisc.git --branch develop

    $ cd lagarto-lowrisc

    $ git submodule update --recursive --init

    $ source set-env.sh # choose (d)efault

    $ source zvivado.sh

    $ cd fpga/board/kc705

    $ make hello -j$(nproc) #could take a while, around 45 minutes

    When it finished you should open a new terminal connect to the server and run an uart terminal client as follows:

    $ picocom -b 115200  --parity o --imap lfcrlf /dev/ttyUSB2

    Then return to the terminal where you compile the project a program the fpga:

   $ make program

    You should see the hello world output in the uart client.

If you wish you can refer to the repository wiki for more details, https://repo.hca.bsc.es/gitlab/lagarto/lagarto-lowrisc/-/wikis/Two-FPGA-Demo-Test, or you can send me an email. I will try to answer as son as possible.

P.D. If you do not have an account in this server let me know and I can create it.


Cheers,

Carlos

